{
  "id": "COMPOUND:18584 - PROTEIN:5793",
  "verbalization": "Zizyberenalic acid is shown to interact with CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase, a protein that plays a critical role in the synthesis of sialic acid, a component of glycoproteins. This enzyme is also targeted by (3aR,6S,6aR)-3,6-bis(3,4,5-trimethoxyphenyl)-1,3,3a,4,6,6a-hexahydrofuro[3,4-c]furan and 6'-O-Z-Para-Methoxycinnamoylharpagide, both of which are implicated in the regulation of hepatic function. The shared target of these compounds with CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase suggests a potential mechanism by which Zizyberenalic acid may influence hepatocyte function. The evidence supports a direct interaction between Zizyberenalic acid and hepatocytes through the modulation of this enzyme, which could have implications for liver function and disease. The consistent targeting of this enzyme across multiple compounds and its role in hepatocyte biology provide a plausible mechanism for the observed relationship.",
